hw/sh4: ze0 DU planes as a QEMU display console (TODO M8.H4).

tools/ze0_screenshot.py copied VRAM with HMP pmemsave and composed the DU
planes on the host, so there was no QEMU console: -display gtk/sdl/vnc and
HMP screendump had nothing to show and run.sh always passed -nographic.

ze0-gfx now optionally registers a graphic console (property display,
default on) named ze0-gfx and composes DU plane 1-4 the way
ze0_screenshot.py compose() does: the plane with the higher number first,
plane 1 on top, a keyed plane (mode bits 0xf000 == 0x1000) drops pixels
with bit 15 clear or the colour key, (A)RGB1555 -> x8r8g8b8.  A keyed
plane whose window band (sx/sy) holds no visible pixel but whose other
480-row page does shows that page, because the NK1 AUI draws into one
page while +0x34 stays on the other (M8.C1t); the host tool does the same.
The frame is recomposed when a 2DG display list ends (SCLR = 1) and on a
console invalidate/gfx_update, so screendump, VNC and a live window show
the same frame the host tool composes.  gfx-display=off is the control for
a run with no console at all.

run.sh gains --display SPEC (none|gtk|sdl|dbus|vnc=HOST:N) and --vnc.  With
a display the machine option gfx-display=on is added and -nographic is
dropped; the debug SCIF still goes to its file.  The default (--display
none) is unchanged.

